---
import Features2 from '~/components/widgets/Features2.astro';
import Hero from '~/components/widgets/Hero.astro';
import Stats from '~/components/widgets/Stats.astro';
import Layout from '~/layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { PLAYGROUND_URL, DOCS_URL } from '~/navigation';

const metadata = {
  title: 'About',
  description:
    'PyMCU compiles a subset of Python ahead of time into native microcontroller assembly: no interpreter, no heap. Why it exists, what it targets, and who builds it.',
};
---

<Layout metadata={metadata}>
  <Hero
    tagline="About"
    image='<img src="/images/pymcu-logo.png" width="512" height="512" alt="The PyMCU logo: a snake coiled over a microcontroller chip" loading="eager" decoding="async" class="mx-auto rounded-md w-full max-w-md" />'
  >
    <Fragment slot="title">
      Python that compiles to <br />
      <span class="text-accent dark:text-white">bare metal</span>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="subtitle">
      MicroPython and CircuitPython bring an interpreter to boards with enough flash and RAM to hold one, and that's
      most of why Python runs on hardware at all. PyMCU takes the other route: it's an ahead-of-time (AOT) compiler,
      so it turns a statically-typed subset of Python straight into native machine code at build time. No
      interpreter, no bytecode, no heap, no garbage collector, no filesystem on the device. That trade reaches boards
      an interpreter can't fit on, like the ATmega328P or an ATtiny, and places where timing has to be exact, like an
      interrupt handler or a bit-banged protocol. The Python you read is the firmware you flash.
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <Stats
    title="A blink on an Arduino Uno"
    subtitle="Measured 2026-09-15 with pymcu-compiler 0.1.0a10."
    stats={[
      { title: 'Code', amount: '40 B' },
      { title: 'RAM used', amount: '0 B' },
      { title: 'Architectures', amount: '4' },
      { title: 'License', amount: 'MIT' },
    ]}
  />

  <Features2
    title="What it actually does"
    subtitle="Every claim here is measured on hardware, not estimated."
    items={[
      {
        title: 'Abstractions that cost nothing',
        description:
          'Pin(13, Pin.OUT) becomes a single SBI instruction. The object is gone before the code reaches the board: classes, properties and drivers resolve at compile time, so you pay in bytes you can count.',
        icon: 'tabler:cpu',
      },
      {
        title: 'Four architectures, one language',
        description:
          'AVR is the mature backend: the Arduino Uno and its family, continuously validated on real silicon. ARM (Raspberry Pi Pico and Pico 2) and 8-bit PIC compile and run today but are alpha. RISC-V (CH32V003) has a working backend in the compiler tree, proven in emulation, not yet published as an installable package. The compiler emits a shared intermediate representation; each backend turns it into assembly for its own silicon.',
        icon: 'tabler:microwave',
      },
      {
        title: 'MicroPython and CircuitPython compatibility layers',
        description:
          "PyMCU replicates the machine and board APIs those two projects define, as libraries the compiler understands. Code written against the parts of that API PyMCU supports carries over: you write the API you already know, and PyMCU compiles it instead of interpreting it.",
        icon: 'tabler:brand-python',
      },
      {
        title: 'Validated on real silicon',
        description:
          'Emulators catch most things and miss the rest. Every release is flashed to physical boards and verified there, because more than one bug has only ever appeared on the real chip.',
        icon: 'tabler:circuit-changeover',
      },
      {
        title: 'Try it without installing anything',
        description: `The playground runs the whole compiler in your browser and simulates the board: write Python, read the assembly it produced, watch the LED blink. ${PLAYGROUND_URL}`,
        icon: 'tabler:player-play',
      },
      {
        title: 'Beta, and honest about it',
        description: `Version 0.1.0b1 is on PyPI: the compiler, the AVR backend and the CircuitPython and MicroPython layers are beta; ARM, PIC and RISC-V stay alpha. The language subset is documented, and so is what it deliberately will not compile: see the docs at ${DOCS_URL}. Expect sharp edges; expect them to be written down.`,
        icon: 'tabler:flask',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <Features2
    title="Who builds it"
    items={[
      {
        title: 'One person',
        description:
          'PyMCU is written and maintained by Iván Montiel Cardona as a personal open-source project, unaffiliated with any employer. It is built in close collaboration with AI tooling: that is how a one-person project sustains a compiler, four backends, emulators and this much testing discipline.',
        icon: 'tabler:user-code',
      },
      {
        title: 'Why it exists',
        description:
          'Because the board that taught millions of people electronics never got to speak Python, and the reason was an implementation detail rather than a law of nature. Compiling instead of interpreting removes it.',
        icon: 'tabler:bulb',
      },
      {
        title: 'How to help',
        description:
          'Try it and report what breaks. A bug found on hardware someone else owns is worth more than any feature request: it is the part a single maintainer cannot cover alone.',
        icon: 'tabler:heart-handshake',
      },
    ]}
  />
</Layout>
